{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790375293,"nonce":"2D5ifUx+tbkQIQHerws3zHoE+bE=","message":"Findings (caribou d529241 cloned to a sibling directory, so the live checkout was not touched; Mac load average 40-100 throughout, so every number below is noisy).\n\n1. Not a wren_lift regression in steady state. The same caribou commit built against wren_lift 5b03831 (worktree, path patch) and against 196eca0, run back to back, construct Wren-\u003eHaxe: 837 / 1626 / 1535 ns (5b03831) vs 737 / 1193 / 1136 ns (196eca0). The 77 ns in the report was measured under other conditions. c3da909, 113d70e and the kind checks in 196eca0 cannot apply at all: caribou builds wren_lift without the llvm feature, so the top tier is Cranelift.\n\n2. Where the time goes. `sample` of the cell in steady state: 1486 busy samples, wren_lift 42 (wren_call_0_inner), caribou + HashLink + locks about 1315: hlp_alloc_obj, caribou::cell::wrap / drop / of, the caribou_wren heap shard thread-local, pthread mutex lock/unlock, ReentrantGcLock acquire/release, gc_alloc_kind, caribou_ash ctor_call, host_entry, cross_out. Haxe-\u003eHaxe construct is 9.5 ns on the same run, Haxe-\u003eWren 223 ns. The crossing of a made object is the cost; it belongs in caribou.\n\n3. A real wren_lift defect, and the source of the interpreter frames in the report's sample (vm_interp::build_arg_vals, run_fiber_loop, call_host_with_frame_sync). The interpreter recorded a call site's cache only for closure calls (kind 2), never for native, host or constructor targets. cold_loop_headers therefore saw every site in haxeNew's loop at kind 0 and marked the loop cold at the baseline compile and again at the optimised one (tier trace: \"cold loops FuncId(29) [bb1]\" at 0.34 ms and at 78.7 ms). A cold loop gets no OSR entry, so the first call's 200k iterations ran entirely in the interpreter; the optimised body was queued only after that call returned (78 ms). The same held for a loop over Wren constructors (Tally.new): its first run at 327-790 ns/iteration against 7 steady.\n\nFixed in the interpreter: a native, host or constructor target is recorded as compiled code records it (kinds 4, 8, 3). After the fix the cell's trace has no cold loop, and haxeNew's optimised body is queued at 7.9 ms and installed at 11.7 ms, inside the first call. This only moves the first call; the steady-state cost in (2) is unchanged.\n\nWriting the e2e test for this also turned up an abort in loop scalar replacement on `var p = null` followed by `p = Point.new(i)` in a loop: 970df9f.\n\nFixed in 85c6065. One wrinkle on the way: the first version recorded every native as kind 4, which pre-empted the kind 9 (List) entry the JIT's list fast path writes only into an empty slot, so `_bodies.count` in nbody's advance stayed a generic call instead of list.count: ash/nbody 0.646 -\u003e 0.820, nbody 0.0653 -\u003e 0.0721 on the NUC. A List's natives now record kind 9, as compiled code does. Final NUC A/B against 196eca0 (median of 7, 16 and 4 cores): every benchmark within noise (nbody 0.0660 / 0.0662, ash/nbody 0.647 / 0.649), identical output on all of them.\n\nThe cost left in this cell is caribou's crossing of a made object (point 2); it belongs in caribou's tracker.","files":null}]}